incident_description:
Sept 27, 2021 - purchased a new Kitchenaid Range Model KFED500ESS from Best Buy for $1980.

July 2022 - plastic part of burner knob that fits onto shaft broke; Kitchenaid replaced;

Oct 18, 2022 - ANOTHER knob broke while the burner was in its hottest position. This time a metal piece of the knob remained on the shaft, making it EXTREMELY difficult to move the shaft to the off position. While trying to remove the metal part and move the control to OFF, it was necessary for my arm to be above the burner. I had moved the sleeve of my top above my elbow to prevent the clothing from catching fire. But the burner was at the highest heat making it very difficult to get into a position with a pair of pliers to remove the broken metal piece from the shaft and turn the burner off.

I bought a Samsung 3-door French door refrigerator from Nebraska Furniture Mart on 11-21-2014 for $2058.20. Shortly after, we noticed that the ice in the door would get jammed in the chute, would melt and stick together and result in water leaking onto our floor. We would have to wait for it to all melt before we could dispense more ice. We didn't think much about it, this being our first refrigerator with the ice-in-door feature. In 2016, our ice maker started completely freezing up and would stop making ice completely. My husband finally decided to reach out to Samsung for support. He called and was told that they could send a tech out to our house to "look at it" but that there would be a charge, the amount they wanted for a service call was about $150.00, outrageous in our opinion. We were also directed to Samsung.com and to follow the troubleshooting options available on there. We would defrost the ice maker per the troubleshooting guidelines every month or so, then it started happening more often, to where we were having to defrost the ice maker every week. This was a huge headache, so in 2017 we decided to just turn off both the top and bottom ice makers and we would just buy bagged ice. We have been buying bagged ice for the past 3 years. Samsung has published service bulletins dating back to 2006 regarding the defective ice maker issue. I know that the NJ AG reached a settlement with Samsung in 2009 for deceptive practices. Samsung agreed to abide by the following terms:
" Under the AVC, Samsung America agreed to:
-Not engage in any unfair acts or deceptive practices in the conduct of its business in the state and to comply with all applicable state and/or federal laws, rules and regulations;
-Provide consumers with all required home appliance warranties and service contracts;
-Disclose to consumers all diagnostic charges and other set fees and the methods to determine the total charge, including the charges for labor and parts, when performing appliance repairs. (Not applicable when Samsung refers out-of-warranty home appliance repairs to an independent service network.)
-Honor all Samsung home appliance warranties and service contracts and to complete all contracted-for, in-warranty repair work, whether completed by Samsung or through an independent service network;
-Provide consumers a toll free customer service telephone number to address any post order inquiries concerning home appliances, and to clearly disclose the telephone numbers to consumers;
-Make available a customer service manager and/or supervisor when the consumer calls the toll-free customer service telephone number and makes such a request.
-Respond to consumer complaints or requests for refunds, credits or other adjustments within five (5) business days of its receipt of the consumerÃÂs telephone call, e mail or letter; and
-Implement training and other programs, as necessary, to instruct its customer service representatives as to new home appliances and/or changes in policies and procedures including, but not limited to, those concerning billing practices"

I believe the way Samsung has handled my complaint is in direct violation of this agreement. I reached out to Samsung Support via the Chat option on January 9th and I was informed by [REDACTED] a Samsung tech pro that he would process a request for a repair Free of Charge. I did not hear from Samsung again until January 14th when I received a text from [REDACTED] Samsung Electronics America stating "Hi [REDACTED]. This is Samsung Electronics America. We like to inform that you may be eligible for accommodation due to no service center in your area to repair your Refrigerator. Please reply to this message with a clear picture of the full purchase receipt if available for us to determine your accommodation option" I replied to the message immediately with my Sales Receipt from Nebraska Furniture Mart for the unit. I did not hear from Samsung again until January 21st. They texted me again from the same number asking me for a ownership certificate or cashier receipt because they could see from my original receipt that the unit was financing on a credit card. I replied an hour later with a credit card statement showing when the unit was paid in full and is owned by me. I received a call on January 25th from "[REDACTED]" at the "Office of the President" with Samsung, which I understand is an external company that Samsung employs to handle these types of requests, and he informed me that due to the age of the unit that I am only eligible for a "pro-rated refund of $800.00" I told him I felt this was insulting and unacceptable. Samsung has known of the defective ice makers on the model of refrigerator I have since 2006, there are also Class Action Lawsuits that are pending against Samsung for this exact defect, proving that Samsung KNOWINGLY sold me a defective product that should have been recalled long before I bought it. I should not have to be punished and not receive a full refund because I had to do my own research to find out that I was sold a defective product. He said that was the best that he could do and ended the conversation. I sent [REDACTED] an email the same day reiterating our conversation and that I want a full refund. I sent him another email on January 27th following up on my refund request, I also informed [REDACTED] that we have been having to buy bagged ice for the past 3 years. We buy (6) 6lb bags of ice per week from our local grocery store that cost $1.69/bag. The grand total for that amount of ice for the past 3 years is: $1581.84!! I told him that I am being more than reasonable for only asking for a refund on my unit and not also asking for reimbursement for the cost of ice that I shouldn't have to be buying.  I also received an email from [REDACTED] at the New Jersey BBB where I have an open case that they are awaiting a response from Samsung. I called 1-800-Samsung on January 30th and spoke with "[REDACTED]" in Case Management, She reviewed my case and again informed me that I am only eligible for a "pro-rated refund" this time of $897.00. I again informed her that is not acceptable for all the reasons listed above. There have been multiple published reports of Samsung making "repairs" on the ice makers, like replacing the motherboards, and the units not keeping steady temperatures below the food-safe requirements. Samsung is selling defective products to their consumers and they are doing illegitimate repairs that are causing more problems and getting people sick with food-borne illnesses and they will do nothing about it!!

Aside from the ice maker issue, I was recently made aware that these defective refrigerators also have issues with temperature regulation. I bought a refrigerator thermometer today and placed it in the center of my refrigerator, per the instructions. I checked the thermometer 4 hours later and I was SHOCKED to see the temperature in our refrigerator is 46 degrees! Our digital display is set on 36 degrees. I have attached photos for your reference. I have noticed in the past that our milk, meat, and produce spoils much quicker than they should. Apparently this is the reason! This is a HUGE safety issue. According to the USDA guidelines, this temperature is unsafe and our food is not safe for consumption. We have been incredibly fortunate that none of us have gotten food poisoning! I have a 2 year old daughter who could get incredibly sick from spoiled food. This is unacceptable and this defective refrigerator is now a hazard to my family's health. Samsung needs to do what is right and provide me a full refund, they are selling unsafe products to their consumers and need to be held accountable!

Kenmore Elite Smoothtop Electric Range
Hazard:
The surface heating elements turned on spontaneously without being switched and fails to turn off after being switched off;

Consumer stated that she is experiencing problems with her front left burner, whenever it is powered off it stays on.  She stated that she has to keep turning it until it finally turns off.  The problem developed about 4-5 days ago.   She stated that she was informed that there had been numerous reports about these incidents with this ranges.

Consumer feels that she might be experiencing similar problems of recall#09-333.  Hazard: Depending on the model, the surface heating elements can: ( 2) fail to turn off after being switched off;

The manufacturer was not contacted as yet.

The caller stated that this coffee carafe was purchased for his church. They have been experiencing the following problems with the unit.


The cap is difficult to screw and become cross-threaded; when pouring cup after cup coffee fills in the cap if the carafe is tip backwards towards the user hand the hot coffee spills out of the space where the thumb lever is located;

When refilling a used carafe coffee in the cap spills out on the surface where it was placed, now the server is faced with cleaning up the the equivalent of half cup of tea or coffee;

The (stopper) that is operated by thumb-lever easily becomes detached and cannot be secured to its original position in the cap.  Consequently the stoppers have been discarded which now allows hot liquids to easily fill the cap as discuss in paragraph 2.

The caller is concerned that these issues are a safety hazard and should be reported.

in kitchen, in the electric range/stove, while deep frying a dish, it started bursting and spilling with flames; in a hurry, took my hand above the dish and switched off the stove; in the process burnt my right hand from below shoulder to the wrist;
even in the normal course, for operating the controls, we have to take the hand above the hot burner/dish on it;
this is because, the controls are situated on the opposite farther side of the stove;
this is too risky;
to avoid the risk, the controls can be provided in front.

1. a description of the consumer product or substance;

KitchenAid KSM158GBCA 90th Anniversary Limited-Edition 5-Quart Stand Mixer, Candy Apple Red

2. the name of the manufacturer, importer, or private labeler of the product;

Kitchen aid

3. a description of an illness, injury, or death, or the risk of illness, injury, or death related to use of the product;

My 3 year old daughter got a laceration requiring 8 stitches on the foot when the glass bowl shattered. It could have been much more dangerous. While the machine was in operation (beating butter and sugar), the accessory screw located on the machine came loose and fell into the bowl, and bowl shattered, and glass pieces flew about. My daughter who was nearby the counter where the stand mixer was injured.

4. the date or estimated date when the incident occurred or when you first became aware of the potential for the product to act in an unsafe manner;

